        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c4c5ce86-216a-5436-bf02-ef6d79c93e76",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Eric Maskin, Leonid Hurwicz, Roger Myerson, Abraham Flexner, Louis Bamberger, Caroline Bamberger Fuld, Peter Sarnak, Charles Loewner, Mikhail Gromov, Michael Freedman, Mikhail Katz, Larry Guth\n- Organizations: Harvard University, Institute for Advanced Study, School of Mathematics\n- Places: Princeton, New Jersey, United States\n- Roles: Adams University Professor, permanent faculty, Nobel laureate\n- Concepts: mechanism design theory, systolic geometry, systolic invariants\n\nFacts:\n- Eric Stark Maskin was born on December 12, 1950.\n- The Institute for Advanced Study was founded in 1930 in Princeton, New Jersey, United States, by Abraham Flexner together with Louis Bamberger and Caroline Bamberger Fuld.\n- Eric Maskin is an American economist and a 2007 Nobel laureate recognized with Leonid Hurwicz and Roger Myerson for laying the foundations of mechanism design theory.\n- Eric Maskin is the Adams University Professor at Harvard University.\n- Peter Sarnak is on the permanent faculty of the School of Mathematics at the Institute for Advanced Study.\n- Systolic geometry studies systolic invariants of manifolds and polyhedra.\n- Systolic geometry was initially conceived by Charles Loewner and developed by Mikhail Gromov, Michael Freedman, Peter Sarnak, Mikhail Katz, Larry Guth, and others.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a3077f21-dce5-527f-9e1e-8b6a4b1ccd8d",
        "text": "Page: Eric Maskin. Sentence: Eric Stark Maskin (born December 12, 1950) is an American economist and 2007 Nobel laureate recognized with Leonid Hurwicz and Roger Myerson \"for having laid the foundations of mechanism design theory\".\nPage: Eric Maskin. Sentence: He is the Adams University Professor at Harvard University.\nPage: Institute for Advanced Study. Sentence: The Institute for Advanced Study (IAS) in Princeton, New Jersey, in the United States, is an independent, postdoctoral research center for theoretical research and intellectual inquiry founded in 1930 by American educator Abraham Flexner, together with philanthropists Louis Bamberger and Caroline Bamberger Fuld.\nPage: Peter Sarnak. Sentence: Sarnak is also on the permanent faculty at the School of Mathematics of the Institute for Advanced Study.\nPage: Systolic geometry. Sentence: In mathematics, systolic geometry is the study of systolic invariants of manifolds and polyhedra, as initially conceived by Charles Loewner and developed by Mikhail Gromov, Michael Freedman, Peter Sarnak, Mikhail Katz, Larry Guth, and others, in its arithmetical, ergodic, and topological manifestations.",
